1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,224
I made a small language model write sixty-four words two ways.

2
00:00:04,224 --> 00:00:05,465
Without one trick,

3
00:00:05,465 --> 00:00:07,722
it took four point two five seconds.

4
00:00:07,722 --> 00:00:08,399
With it,

5
00:00:08,399 --> 00:00:09,584
point three five.

6
00:00:09,584 --> 00:00:10,938
Twelve times faster,

7
00:00:10,938 --> 00:00:11,785
same model,

8
00:00:11,785 --> 00:00:12,687
same answer.

9
00:00:13,037 --> 00:00:15,740
That trick is the K V cache from lesson one,

10
00:00:15,740 --> 00:00:18,669
and it is only the first of four in this lesson.

11
00:00:18,669 --> 00:00:21,203
We will also measure continuous batching,

12
00:00:21,203 --> 00:00:23,568
compression, and speculative decoding.

13
00:00:23,568 --> 00:00:26,834
By the end you will know how a trained model is served

14
00:00:26,834 --> 00:00:27,341
fast.

15
00:00:28,591 --> 00:00:31,941
Welcome to lesson six of the cloud for A I engineers course,

16
00:00:31,941 --> 00:00:33,250
on The Code Notebook.

17
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:36,734
The handwritten notes and the code for this lesson are linked in

18
00:00:36,734 --> 00:00:37,656
the description.

19
00:00:38,906 --> 00:00:40,619
Lesson five trained a model.

20
00:00:40,619 --> 00:00:41,582
Now we use it,

21
00:00:41,582 --> 00:00:44,097
which the field calls inference or serving.

22
00:00:44,097 --> 00:00:45,327
For most companies,

23
00:00:45,327 --> 00:00:47,628
this is where the money goes every day,

24
00:00:47,628 --> 00:00:49,394
so it is worth getting right.

25
00:00:49,744 --> 00:00:50,575
As always,

26
00:00:50,575 --> 00:00:52,177
I label what I measure.

27
00:00:52,177 --> 00:00:54,728
The prefill and cache tests run a real,

28
00:00:54,728 --> 00:00:58,289
small transformer with random weights on one CPU thread.

29
00:00:58,289 --> 00:00:59,653
They measure speed,

30
00:00:59,653 --> 00:01:00,603
not quality.

31
00:01:00,603 --> 00:01:03,985
The batching and speculation results are simulations,

32
00:01:03,985 --> 00:01:07,071
and the compression test uses synthetic weights.

33
00:01:08,321 --> 00:01:10,246
Every answer has two phases.

34
00:01:10,246 --> 00:01:14,396
Imagine being handed a long question and asked to reply out loud.

35
00:01:14,396 --> 00:01:17,162
First you read the whole question at once.

36
00:01:17,162 --> 00:01:18,365
That is prefill.

37
00:01:18,365 --> 00:01:20,831
All the words are processed together,

38
00:01:20,831 --> 00:01:23,538
which suits a GPU's thousands of workers.

39
00:01:23,538 --> 00:01:24,981
It is compute bound.

40
00:01:25,331 --> 00:01:28,043
Then you speak the reply one word at a time.

41
00:01:28,043 --> 00:01:29,116
That is decode.

42
00:01:29,116 --> 00:01:31,262
Each word needs the one before it,

43
00:01:31,262 --> 00:01:33,352
so it cannot be done in parallel,

44
00:01:33,352 --> 00:01:36,120
and each step re reads the model from memory.

45
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:37,419
It is memory bound,

46
00:01:37,419 --> 00:01:39,001
as we saw in lesson one.

47
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:40,789
Start fast is prefill.

48
00:01:40,789 --> 00:01:42,116
Talk fast is decode.

49
00:01:42,116 --> 00:01:43,775
They need different fixes.

50
00:01:45,025 --> 00:01:47,666
So we measure speed with several clocks.

51
00:01:47,666 --> 00:01:48,386
T T F T,

52
00:01:48,386 --> 00:01:49,826
time to first token,

53
00:01:49,826 --> 00:01:52,587
is the wait before the first word appears.

54
00:01:52,587 --> 00:01:54,627
It is queue time plus prefill.

55
00:01:54,977 --> 00:01:55,733
T P O T,

56
00:01:55,733 --> 00:01:56,552
or I T L,

57
00:01:56,552 --> 00:01:59,072
is the gap between words after that.

58
00:01:59,072 --> 00:02:02,033
It decides whether the stream feels smooth.

59
00:02:02,033 --> 00:02:05,372
Throughput is tokens per second across all users.

60
00:02:05,722 --> 00:02:09,425
And goodput is the number of requests per second that meet both

61
00:02:09,425 --> 00:02:10,530
targets at once.

62
00:02:10,530 --> 00:02:12,243
Size your fleet by goodput.

63
00:02:12,243 --> 00:02:15,393
Raw throughput can always be bought with worse waits.

64
00:02:16,643 --> 00:02:18,804
A simple sum ties them together.

65
00:02:18,804 --> 00:02:22,585
Total time is about the first token wait plus the number of

66
00:02:22,585 --> 00:02:25,466
remaining tokens times the gap between them.

67
00:02:25,466 --> 00:02:29,787
A five hundred token answer with a twenty millisecond gap spends ten

68
00:02:29,787 --> 00:02:31,287
seconds just writing.

69
00:02:31,637 --> 00:02:35,402
That is why people stream tokens to the screen as they appear.

70
00:02:35,402 --> 00:02:38,539
The user starts reading after the first token wait,

71
00:02:38,539 --> 00:02:40,193
not after the last token.

72
00:02:41,443 --> 00:02:43,176
Why is decode the slow part?

73
00:02:43,176 --> 00:02:46,641
Each new word re reads every weight and the whole cache from

74
00:02:46,641 --> 00:02:49,565
memory, but does very little arithmetic with them.

75
00:02:49,565 --> 00:02:51,135
The chip waits on memory.

76
00:02:51,485 --> 00:02:52,839
On an H one hundred,

77
00:02:52,839 --> 00:02:56,224
a decode step at batch one uses about one percent of the

78
00:02:56,224 --> 00:02:57,465
chip's arithmetic.

79
00:02:57,465 --> 00:02:59,158
More flops would not help.

80
00:02:59,158 --> 00:03:00,174
Faster memory,

81
00:03:00,174 --> 00:03:01,076
fewer bytes,

82
00:03:01,076 --> 00:03:03,841
or sharing each read across more users would.

83
00:03:05,091 --> 00:03:06,351
Let me measure it.

84
00:03:06,351 --> 00:03:09,271
I wrote a small transformer by hand in PyTorch.

85
00:03:09,271 --> 00:03:10,130
Six layers,

86
00:03:10,130 --> 00:03:12,363
three hundred and eighty-four wide,

87
00:03:12,363 --> 00:03:14,596
about seventeen million parameters,

88
00:03:14,596 --> 00:03:15,970
with random weights.

89
00:03:15,970 --> 00:03:17,573
Random weights are fine,

90
00:03:17,573 --> 00:03:18,947
because I am timing,

91
00:03:18,947 --> 00:03:20,322
not judging answers.

92
00:03:20,672 --> 00:03:21,913
One CPU thread,

93
00:03:21,913 --> 00:03:23,153
full precision,

94
00:03:23,153 --> 00:03:24,852
best of several tries.

95
00:03:24,852 --> 00:03:26,354
A CPU is not a GPU,

96
00:03:26,354 --> 00:03:28,444
but the shape of the result,

97
00:03:28,444 --> 00:03:31,187
prefill is parallel and decode is not,

98
00:03:31,187 --> 00:03:32,232
is the same.

99
00:03:33,482 --> 00:03:34,641
First, prefill.

100
00:03:34,641 --> 00:03:38,851
A sixty-four token prompt took twenty-two point six milliseconds.

101
00:03:38,851 --> 00:03:42,756
Two hundred and fifty-six tokens took sixty-two point eight.

102
00:03:42,756 --> 00:03:46,661
A thousand and twenty-four took two hundred and sixty-seven.

103
00:03:47,011 --> 00:03:47,788
Per token,

104
00:03:47,788 --> 00:03:50,284
that is about a quarter of a millisecond.

105
00:03:50,284 --> 00:03:52,392
Longer prompts cost more in total,

106
00:03:52,392 --> 00:03:55,277
so time to first token grows with prompt length.

107
00:03:56,527 --> 00:03:58,373
Now compare the two phases.

108
00:03:58,373 --> 00:04:01,828
Prefill processed about four thousand tokens a second.

109
00:04:01,828 --> 00:04:04,746
Decode, one token per step even with a cache,

110
00:04:04,746 --> 00:04:07,306
managed about a hundred and eighty-two.

111
00:04:07,656 --> 00:04:11,482
A decode token costs roughly nineteen times a prefill token.

112
00:04:11,482 --> 00:04:12,379
Same model,

113
00:04:12,379 --> 00:04:13,396
same machine.

114
00:04:13,396 --> 00:04:14,830
Prefill is parallel,

115
00:04:14,830 --> 00:04:16,325
decode waits in line.

116
00:04:17,575 --> 00:04:18,580
Now the cache.

117
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:21,484
When the model writes word number three hundred,

118
00:04:21,484 --> 00:04:24,723
it needs the keys and values of all the earlier words.

119
00:04:24,723 --> 00:04:25,840
Without a cache,

120
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:29,414
it recomputes all of them from scratch at every single step.

121
00:04:29,764 --> 00:04:30,733
With a cache,

122
00:04:30,733 --> 00:04:33,185
the earlier keys and values are stored,

123
00:04:33,185 --> 00:04:35,693
so each step computes only the new word.

124
00:04:35,693 --> 00:04:38,658
It is the waiter with a notepad from lesson one.

125
00:04:38,658 --> 00:04:40,654
I tested both on the same task.

126
00:04:41,904 --> 00:04:43,003
With the cache,

127
00:04:43,003 --> 00:04:46,013
sixty-four tokens took point three five seconds,

128
00:04:46,013 --> 00:04:48,964
about a hundred and eighty-two tokens a second.

129
00:04:48,964 --> 00:04:49,832
Without it,

130
00:04:49,832 --> 00:04:51,684
four point two five seconds,

131
00:04:51,684 --> 00:04:53,305
fifteen tokens a second.

132
00:04:53,655 --> 00:04:55,485
Twelve point one times slower.

133
00:04:55,485 --> 00:04:58,014
And the gap would grow with longer answers,

134
00:04:58,014 --> 00:05:01,297
because the no cache version re does more work each step.

135
00:05:02,547 --> 00:05:04,067
The cache is not free.

136
00:05:04,067 --> 00:05:05,178
It uses memory.

137
00:05:05,178 --> 00:05:07,635
Does the formula from lesson one hold?

138
00:05:07,635 --> 00:05:11,202
Two times layers times heads times head size times bytes.

139
00:05:11,202 --> 00:05:12,196
For my model,

140
00:05:12,196 --> 00:05:15,939
that is two times six times six times sixty-four times four,

141
00:05:15,939 --> 00:05:17,226
eighteen thousand,

142
00:05:17,226 --> 00:05:20,033
four hundred and thirty-two bytes per token.

143
00:05:20,383 --> 00:05:24,268
I added up the real tensors after a two hundred and fifty-six

144
00:05:24,268 --> 00:05:25,284
token prompt.

145
00:05:25,284 --> 00:05:27,376
Four point seven million bytes,

146
00:05:27,376 --> 00:05:30,185
which divides to exactly eighteen thousand,

147
00:05:30,185 --> 00:05:32,695
four hundred and thirty-two per token.

148
00:05:32,695 --> 00:05:34,189
The formula is right.

149
00:05:35,439 --> 00:05:37,696
For a big model the cache is serious.

150
00:05:37,696 --> 00:05:40,888
Take a seventy billion class model with eighty layers,

151
00:05:40,888 --> 00:05:42,099
eight cache heads,

152
00:05:42,099 --> 00:05:44,630
and heads a hundred and twenty-eight wide.

153
00:05:44,630 --> 00:05:48,098
That is about three hundred and twenty kilobytes per token.

154
00:05:48,098 --> 00:05:51,951
An eight thousand token chat holds about two and a half gigabytes.

155
00:05:52,301 --> 00:05:56,048
If the model kept a separate cache head for every attention head,

156
00:05:56,048 --> 00:05:57,677
as the oldest designs did,

157
00:05:57,677 --> 00:05:59,687
that would be eight times bigger,

158
00:05:59,687 --> 00:06:00,827
twenty gigabytes.

159
00:06:00,827 --> 00:06:02,946
Sharing cache heads between groups,

160
00:06:02,946 --> 00:06:04,846
called grouped query attention,

161
00:06:04,846 --> 00:06:06,747
is why modern models use eight.

162
00:06:07,097 --> 00:06:09,150
A compressed design from DeepSeek,

163
00:06:09,150 --> 00:06:10,825
and smaller number formats,

164
00:06:10,825 --> 00:06:11,852
cut it further.

165
00:06:11,852 --> 00:06:14,391
These sizes come from the notebook's table,

166
00:06:14,391 --> 00:06:15,363
as arithmetic.

167
00:06:16,613 --> 00:06:18,460
Capacity planning follows.

168
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:22,954
Two eighty gigabyte GPUs holding a seventy billion model in eight bit

169
00:06:22,954 --> 00:06:26,094
format use about seventy gigabytes for weights.

170
00:06:26,094 --> 00:06:29,542
That leaves roughly seventy to eighty for the cache.

171
00:06:29,892 --> 00:06:32,897
At three hundred and twenty kilobytes per token,

172
00:06:32,897 --> 00:06:36,191
that is around thirty chats of eight thousand tokens.

173
00:06:36,191 --> 00:06:39,254
Storing the cache in eight bit format doubles it.

174
00:06:39,254 --> 00:06:42,780
That is the first lever to pull before you buy more GPUs.

175
00:06:44,030 --> 00:06:45,654
Two more cache tricks.

176
00:06:45,654 --> 00:06:49,529
Old servers reserved one long block of memory per request,

177
00:06:49,529 --> 00:06:52,028
even though most of it stayed empty.

178
00:06:52,028 --> 00:06:53,903
PagedAttention, from vLLM,

179
00:06:53,903 --> 00:06:56,215
hands out small blocks on demand,

180
00:06:56,215 --> 00:06:58,590
like pages in an operating system.

181
00:06:58,590 --> 00:07:01,339
Waste drops to under one block per chat.

182
00:07:01,689 --> 00:07:02,989
And prefix caching.

183
00:07:02,989 --> 00:07:06,096
If many requests start with the same system prompt,

184
00:07:06,096 --> 00:07:08,357
they share the same keys and values,

185
00:07:08,357 --> 00:07:10,560
so that part of prefill is skipped.

186
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:12,199
Put shared content first,

187
00:07:12,199 --> 00:07:13,781
byte for byte identical,

188
00:07:13,781 --> 00:07:15,815
and your first token wait falls.

189
00:07:17,065 --> 00:07:17,998
Now batching,

190
00:07:17,998 --> 00:07:19,697
the biggest lever for cost.

191
00:07:19,697 --> 00:07:21,781
There are two ways to run a batch.

192
00:07:21,781 --> 00:07:24,304
Static batching takes thirty-two requests,

193
00:07:24,304 --> 00:07:25,565
runs them together,

194
00:07:25,565 --> 00:07:29,897
and waits until the slowest one finishes before taking the next thirty-two.

195
00:07:29,897 --> 00:07:32,255
Short answers sit finished but waiting.

196
00:07:32,605 --> 00:07:36,082
Continuous batching re forms the batch after every step.

197
00:07:36,082 --> 00:07:37,820
When one request finishes,

198
00:07:37,820 --> 00:07:40,544
a waiting one takes its slot straight away.

199
00:07:40,544 --> 00:07:41,761
I simulated both.

200
00:07:41,761 --> 00:07:43,267
Two thousand requests,

201
00:07:43,267 --> 00:07:44,484
thirty-two slots,

202
00:07:44,484 --> 00:07:46,512
a twenty-five millisecond step,

203
00:07:46,512 --> 00:07:48,077
which is my assumption,

204
00:07:48,077 --> 00:07:52,365
and random answer lengths averaging about a hundred and twenty tokens.

205
00:07:53,615 --> 00:07:57,766
Static batching delivered three hundred and thirty-five tokens a second.

206
00:07:57,766 --> 00:07:59,077
Continuous batching,

207
00:07:59,077 --> 00:08:00,988
twelve hundred and forty-three.

208
00:08:01,338 --> 00:08:04,881
Three point seven times the output from the same GPU,

209
00:08:04,881 --> 00:08:08,734
because no slot sat idle waiting for a long answer to end.

210
00:08:09,984 --> 00:08:11,884
It improves waiting time too.

211
00:08:11,884 --> 00:08:13,324
With static batching,

212
00:08:13,324 --> 00:08:17,641
the average request finished after three hundred and sixty-six seconds.

213
00:08:17,641 --> 00:08:19,311
With continuous batching,

214
00:08:19,311 --> 00:08:20,232
ninety-five.

215
00:08:20,582 --> 00:08:24,758
Short requests no longer wait behind the longest one in their batch.

216
00:08:24,758 --> 00:08:28,760
That is why every modern serving engine uses continuous batching.

217
00:08:30,010 --> 00:08:31,268
One flaw remains.

218
00:08:31,268 --> 00:08:35,164
A long prompt's prefill is a big chunk of arithmetic that can

219
00:08:35,164 --> 00:08:36,422
hog a whole step.

220
00:08:36,422 --> 00:08:39,718
Every other user's reply freezes until it finishes,

221
00:08:39,718 --> 00:08:41,756
a stall that can last seconds.

222
00:08:42,106 --> 00:08:43,850
The fix is chunked prefill.

223
00:08:43,850 --> 00:08:45,988
Give each step a budget of tokens.

224
00:08:45,988 --> 00:08:48,407
Fill it with the ongoing decodes first,

225
00:08:48,407 --> 00:08:50,658
then add a slice of the long prompt.

226
00:08:50,658 --> 00:08:52,852
The gaps between words stay smooth,

227
00:08:52,852 --> 00:08:56,396
at the price of a slightly longer first token wait for that

228
00:08:56,396 --> 00:08:57,297
long prompt.

229
00:08:58,547 --> 00:09:01,238
Big deployments go one step further.

230
00:09:01,238 --> 00:09:06,216
Disaggregation. Run prefill on one pool of GPUs and decode on another.

231
00:09:06,216 --> 00:09:09,378
Prefill machines are chosen for arithmetic.

232
00:09:09,378 --> 00:09:12,741
Decode machines for memory speed and capacity.

233
00:09:13,091 --> 00:09:16,333
The cache must travel between them over a fast link.

234
00:09:16,333 --> 00:09:18,244
It pays off for long prompts,

235
00:09:18,244 --> 00:09:21,949
strict smoothness targets and big mixture of experts models.

236
00:09:21,949 --> 00:09:23,917
It does not pay on a few GPUs,

237
00:09:23,917 --> 00:09:26,001
where chunked prefill is enough.

238
00:09:27,251 --> 00:09:29,811
Which software serves the model?

239
00:09:29,811 --> 00:09:31,518
vLLM is the default.

240
00:09:31,518 --> 00:09:36,283
It supports the widest range of models and chips and speaks the

241
00:09:36,283 --> 00:09:38,203
OpenAI style interface.

242
00:09:38,203 --> 00:09:41,759
SGLang is strong when requests share prefixes,

243
00:09:41,759 --> 00:09:42,968
as in agents.

244
00:09:42,968 --> 00:09:47,378
TensorRT LLM squeezes the last few percent on NVIDIA only.

245
00:09:47,728 --> 00:09:51,715
Dynamo and llm d add multi machine routing on top.

246
00:09:51,715 --> 00:09:53,930
Hugging Face's old server,

247
00:09:53,930 --> 00:09:54,668
T G I,

248
00:09:54,668 --> 00:09:56,661
is in maintenance mode,

249
00:09:56,661 --> 00:10:00,501
and its own advice is to move to vLLM or SGLang.

250
00:10:00,501 --> 00:10:01,756
For a laptop,

251
00:10:01,756 --> 00:10:02,568
Ollama.

252
00:10:03,818 --> 00:10:06,160
Here is what a real launch looks like,

253
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:07,053
as a sketch.

254
00:10:07,053 --> 00:10:08,056
Serve a model,

255
00:10:08,056 --> 00:10:09,506
split across two GPUs,

256
00:10:09,506 --> 00:10:11,235
store weights in eight bit,

257
00:10:11,235 --> 00:10:13,187
and the cache in eight bit too.

258
00:10:13,187 --> 00:10:14,302
Cap the context,

259
00:10:14,302 --> 00:10:16,812
and tell it how much memory it may claim.

260
00:10:17,162 --> 00:10:18,113
The last flag,

261
00:10:18,113 --> 00:10:19,646
the batched token budget,

262
00:10:19,646 --> 00:10:21,337
is the chunked prefill knob.

263
00:10:21,337 --> 00:10:23,082
Higher gives more throughput,

264
00:10:23,082 --> 00:10:24,667
lower gives smoother gaps.

265
00:10:24,667 --> 00:10:26,834
Check the flags against your version,

266
00:10:26,834 --> 00:10:28,420
because they change often.

267
00:10:29,670 --> 00:10:30,779
Now compression,

268
00:10:30,779 --> 00:10:32,110
called quantisation.

269
00:10:32,110 --> 00:10:35,104
Decode time is proportional to the bytes you read,

270
00:10:35,104 --> 00:10:38,930
so storing weights in eight bits roughly halves the time per word

271
00:10:38,930 --> 00:10:40,150
when memory bound,

272
00:10:40,150 --> 00:10:42,257
and four bits roughly quarters it.

273
00:10:42,257 --> 00:10:44,642
It also leaves more room for the cache.

274
00:10:44,992 --> 00:10:46,116
Common formats.

275
00:10:46,116 --> 00:10:49,905
Eight bit float is the near lossless default on newer chips.

276
00:10:49,905 --> 00:10:52,747
Four bit integer methods store weights only,

277
00:10:52,747 --> 00:10:54,819
so the maths stays sixteen bit.

278
00:10:54,819 --> 00:10:58,845
The newest four bit float format on Blackwell does true four bit

279
00:10:58,845 --> 00:10:59,437
maths.

280
00:11:00,687 --> 00:11:02,931
I tested what plain rounding does.

281
00:11:02,931 --> 00:11:07,302
A thousand by thousand weight matrix with a few outlier columns twenty

282
00:11:07,302 --> 00:11:08,306
times larger,

283
00:11:08,306 --> 00:11:11,436
because large models do have a few such channels.

284
00:11:11,436 --> 00:11:13,090
The matrix is synthetic,

285
00:11:13,090 --> 00:11:14,389
my own assumption.

286
00:11:14,739 --> 00:11:17,022
I rounded it to eight and four bits,

287
00:11:17,022 --> 00:11:17,878
three ways.

288
00:11:17,878 --> 00:11:19,874
One scale for the whole matrix,

289
00:11:19,874 --> 00:11:20,787
one per row,

290
00:11:20,787 --> 00:11:23,298
and one per group of sixty-four weights.

291
00:11:23,298 --> 00:11:27,292
Then I multiplied by random inputs and measured how far the output

292
00:11:27,292 --> 00:11:27,862
moved.

293
00:11:29,112 --> 00:11:32,899
Eight bits with one scale for the whole matrix moved the output

294
00:11:32,899 --> 00:11:34,708
by eight point four percent.

295
00:11:34,708 --> 00:11:35,952
One scale per row,

296
00:11:35,952 --> 00:11:37,026
four point two.

297
00:11:37,026 --> 00:11:39,174
One scale per group of sixty-four,

298
00:11:39,174 --> 00:11:40,248
one point four.

299
00:11:40,598 --> 00:11:43,591
Four bits with a single scale was a disaster,

300
00:11:43,591 --> 00:11:44,936
fifty-one percent.

301
00:11:44,936 --> 00:11:48,113
Even groups of sixty-four left nineteen percent.

302
00:11:48,113 --> 00:11:50,984
Plain four bit rounding is not good enough.

303
00:11:50,984 --> 00:11:53,550
Real methods such as A W Q and G P T Q

304
00:11:53,550 --> 00:11:54,589
are cleverer,

305
00:11:54,589 --> 00:11:56,605
and I did not test them here.

306
00:11:57,855 --> 00:11:58,845
A last trick.

307
00:11:58,845 --> 00:12:00,827
Decode leaves arithmetic idle,

308
00:12:00,827 --> 00:12:01,643
so use it.

309
00:12:01,643 --> 00:12:04,964
A small cheap draft model guesses the next few words.

310
00:12:04,964 --> 00:12:08,053
The big model checks all the guesses in one pass,

311
00:12:08,053 --> 00:12:09,568
keeps the correct run,

312
00:12:09,568 --> 00:12:10,850
and adds one more.

313
00:12:10,850 --> 00:12:14,813
The final text is exactly what the big model would have written.

314
00:12:15,163 --> 00:12:18,738
The speed up depends on how often guesses are accepted.

315
00:12:18,738 --> 00:12:21,950
If each guess is accepted with probability alpha,

316
00:12:21,950 --> 00:12:25,767
the expected words per big model pass is one minus alpha to

317
00:12:25,767 --> 00:12:27,706
the power of gamma plus one,

318
00:12:27,706 --> 00:12:29,221
over one minus alpha.

319
00:12:29,221 --> 00:12:31,342
Gamma is the number of guesses.

320
00:12:32,592 --> 00:12:36,605
I simulated two hundred thousand passes and the formula matched.

321
00:12:36,605 --> 00:12:39,202
With alpha point seven and five guesses,

322
00:12:39,202 --> 00:12:42,980
the big model gets about two point nine five words per pass.

323
00:12:42,980 --> 00:12:46,285
Assuming the draft costs five percent of a big step,

324
00:12:46,285 --> 00:12:49,236
that is a two point three five times speed up.

325
00:12:49,586 --> 00:12:51,333
At alpha point five five,

326
00:12:51,333 --> 00:12:53,262
one point seven three times.

327
00:12:53,262 --> 00:12:54,347
At point nine,

328
00:12:54,347 --> 00:12:55,974
three point seven five.

329
00:12:55,974 --> 00:12:59,288
Everything depends on how predictable your text is.

330
00:13:00,538 --> 00:13:02,719
A reality check from the notebook.

331
00:13:02,719 --> 00:13:03,636
In practice,

332
00:13:03,636 --> 00:13:07,824
speculative decoding gives about two to three times on a small model,

333
00:13:07,824 --> 00:13:09,947
and only when the batch is small.

334
00:13:09,947 --> 00:13:13,733
At large batch sizes decode is already near the compute limit,

335
00:13:13,733 --> 00:13:15,168
so the benefit fades.

336
00:13:15,518 --> 00:13:19,292
One test of an eight billion model got one point eight times

337
00:13:19,292 --> 00:13:20,000
on code,

338
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:21,828
and no gain at all on chat.

339
00:13:21,828 --> 00:13:25,603
If the acceptance rate falls below about fifty-five percent,

340
00:13:25,603 --> 00:13:26,842
it is a net loss.

341
00:13:26,842 --> 00:13:28,906
Measure it on your own traffic.

342
00:13:30,156 --> 00:13:31,770
One more building block.

343
00:13:31,770 --> 00:13:35,112
Attention is the part that gets slow with long inputs.

344
00:13:35,112 --> 00:13:39,031
Flash attention rewrites it so the huge table of scores is never

345
00:13:39,031 --> 00:13:40,241
stored in memory.

346
00:13:40,241 --> 00:13:41,566
The newest version,

347
00:13:41,566 --> 00:13:43,295
built for Blackwell chips,

348
00:13:43,295 --> 00:13:47,271
reports up to about sixteen hundred teraflops on a B two hundred.

349
00:13:47,621 --> 00:13:49,505
You rarely write this yourself.

350
00:13:49,505 --> 00:13:51,712
Serving engines switch it on for you.

351
00:13:51,712 --> 00:13:55,427
What matters is knowing it is why long contexts are affordable at

352
00:13:55,427 --> 00:13:55,857
all.

353
00:13:57,107 --> 00:13:59,178
Should you run all this yourself?

354
00:13:59,178 --> 00:14:00,800
Managed A P Is bundle it.

355
00:14:00,800 --> 00:14:02,199
They offer on demand,

356
00:14:02,199 --> 00:14:04,158
batch jobs at about half price,

357
00:14:04,158 --> 00:14:05,221
priority tiers,

358
00:14:05,221 --> 00:14:08,858
and prompt caching where repeated input costs around a tenth.

359
00:14:09,208 --> 00:14:11,321
Self hosting wins only at high,

360
00:14:11,321 --> 00:14:12,227
steady use,

361
00:14:12,227 --> 00:14:14,762
because an idle GPU still costs money.

362
00:14:14,762 --> 00:14:17,842
Lesson eleven does the break even sum properly.

363
00:14:17,842 --> 00:14:21,706
For now remember that the tricks in this lesson are what the

364
00:14:21,706 --> 00:14:23,758
providers use to price so low.

365
00:14:25,008 --> 00:14:26,506
For ShopNest Copilot.

366
00:14:26,506 --> 00:14:29,081
Serve an eight billion model with vLLM.

367
00:14:29,081 --> 00:14:30,998
Eight bit weights and cache,

368
00:14:30,998 --> 00:14:36,029
prefix caching on because every request starts with the same store instructions,

369
00:14:36,029 --> 00:14:38,964
and continuous batching with chunked prefill.

370
00:14:39,314 --> 00:14:43,294
Then measure first token wait and the gap between words under our

371
00:14:43,294 --> 00:14:44,275
real traffic,

372
00:14:44,275 --> 00:14:47,736
and add speculation only if the acceptance rate is good.

373
00:14:47,736 --> 00:14:49,178
Measure, then decide.

374
00:14:50,428 --> 00:14:52,436
Four words to carry forward.

375
00:14:52,436 --> 00:14:53,189
T T F T:

376
00:14:53,189 --> 00:14:55,198
the wait for the first word.

377
00:14:55,198 --> 00:14:56,076
K V cache:

378
00:14:56,076 --> 00:15:00,030
stored keys and values so earlier words are not recomputed.

379
00:15:00,380 --> 00:15:01,773
Continuous batching:

380
00:15:01,773 --> 00:15:04,560
refill a slot the moment a request finishes.

381
00:15:04,560 --> 00:15:05,779
And quantisation:

382
00:15:05,779 --> 00:15:07,288
fewer bits per number,

383
00:15:07,288 --> 00:15:09,494
for less memory and faster decode.

384
00:15:10,744 --> 00:15:12,077
Interview corner.

385
00:15:12,077 --> 00:15:14,869
Asked why decode is slower than prefill,

386
00:15:14,869 --> 00:15:19,819
say: prefill processes all prompt tokens in parallel and is compute bound,

387
00:15:19,819 --> 00:15:23,373
decode makes one token per step and is memory bound.

388
00:15:23,373 --> 00:15:24,325
In my test,

389
00:15:24,325 --> 00:15:27,753
a decode token cost about nineteen prefill tokens.

390
00:15:28,103 --> 00:15:30,049
Asked how to raise throughput,

391
00:15:30,049 --> 00:15:31,710
say: continuous batching,

392
00:15:31,710 --> 00:15:32,969
a quantised cache,

393
00:15:32,969 --> 00:15:34,286
and prefix caching.

394
00:15:34,286 --> 00:15:35,489
In my simulation,

395
00:15:35,489 --> 00:15:39,897
continuous batching gave three point seven times the tokens per second of

396
00:15:39,897 --> 00:15:41,043
static batching.

397
00:15:42,293 --> 00:15:43,048
Your turn.

398
00:15:43,048 --> 00:15:45,478
The zip for this lesson has four scripts.

399
00:15:45,478 --> 00:15:47,152
The first measures prefill,

400
00:15:47,152 --> 00:15:49,581
decode and the cache on your own machine.

401
00:15:49,581 --> 00:15:52,227
The others simulate batching and speculation,

402
00:15:52,227 --> 00:15:53,576
and test compression.

403
00:15:53,926 --> 00:15:57,408
Change the answer length or the number of slots and watch the

404
00:15:57,408 --> 00:15:58,318
numbers move.

405
00:15:58,318 --> 00:16:01,531
One changed number teaches more than an hour of reading.

406
00:16:02,781 --> 00:16:04,938
Recap. Answers have two phases,

407
00:16:04,938 --> 00:16:08,820
prefill which is parallel and decode which waits on memory.

408
00:16:08,820 --> 00:16:12,393
The K V cache made my small model twelve times faster,

409
00:16:12,393 --> 00:16:14,673
and its size matches the formula.

410
00:16:14,673 --> 00:16:19,726
Continuous batching gave three point seven times the throughput in simulation.

411
00:16:20,076 --> 00:16:22,589
Plain four bit rounding broke a matrix,

412
00:16:22,589 --> 00:16:24,518
eight bit per group was fine,

413
00:16:24,518 --> 00:16:28,025
and speculation helps most when guesses are predictable.

414
00:16:28,025 --> 00:16:28,960
Next lesson:

415
00:16:28,960 --> 00:16:29,661
M L Ops.

416
00:16:29,661 --> 00:16:31,707
